FreeSDN SLA measurement boundaries
=================================

A displayed policy compliance percentage is the fraction of its configured,
measured targets that pass at one evaluation. It is not time-based availability,
an error budget, a service credit calculation or a contractual SLA certificate.

Current observations
--------------------
* Site and organization scopes: composite device health score, including zero.
  A composite health score is not measured uptime or client satisfaction.
* Camera and NVR scopes: the fraction of collected camera-health snapshots that
  report online within the configured window. This is sampled availability;
  missing polls, coverage gaps and irregular sampling do not establish uptime.
* Other scope/metric combinations without an implemented collector remain
  unavailable. Selecting a threshold does not create its observation source.

Missing data
------------
Every configured non-null target must have a finite observation for a numeric
whole-policy percentage. Missing observations clear the current percentage to
null (N/A in the UI). An incomplete evaluation does not write a numeric history
snapshot, count a missing target as passing, or resolve that target's existing
incident. Other measured targets can still create or resolve incidents.
A policy with no targets is also unavailable. A measured zero is a valid value.
The evaluation timestamp records an attempt; it is not proof of sensor freshness.

Upgrade implications
--------------------
Earlier versions used composite health as site/organization uptime and treated
unavailable targets as passing. Existing policies are preserved, but their next
active evaluation can become N/A. Review each threshold with the operator and
use health_score_min for composite health. New site policies do not silently
include an uptime target. Old snapshots, reports and disabled-policy cached
values are retained as historical records, not recomputed or certified.

Incident lifecycle
------------------
Acknowledged incidents remain unresolved until a measured target recovers. They
are updated without creating a replacement, and recovery closes every retained
duplicate from earlier versions. Acknowledging an already resolved incident does
not reopen it. Overlapping evaluators skip policies locked by the current pass;
policies_evaluated reports the work that this pass actually performed.

Consecutive confirmation
------------------------
The configured breach_after_consecutive counts repeated violations in complete
policy evaluations. Pending violations affect the measured compliance percentage,
but a new incident is raised only when its own metric reaches the configured count.
Counters persist in snapshot metadata, capped at the configured count. Missing or
partial evaluations, compliant observations, target/configuration changes and
disabling/resuming a policy break the sequence. Legacy snapshots without counter
metadata start a new sequence. Existing unresolved incidents continue to be tracked.
These are evaluations, not proof of independent or fresh physical observations;
manual evaluation also counts. No availability coverage guarantee is implied.

Remaining qualification
-----------------------
Health-observation freshness, fleet coverage, full-window availability, pre-breach
warning-threshold behavior and multi-host operational behavior
need independent acceptance before contractual or enterprise SLA claims. A report
can average only the complete snapshots that exist; it does not interpolate
missing intervals or certify that every scheduled evaluation occurred.
